WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.520
An ongoing crisis and a tragic loss have affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:08.200
the "Pawn Stars" family. Rick Harrison&nbsp;
has finally opened up about what happened.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:15.240
On January 19, 2024, "Pawn Stars" lead Rick&nbsp;
Harrison's son Adam passed away. He was 39 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:21.280
old. While initial reports suggested an overdose,&nbsp;
details at the time were scarce. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.680
Rick Harrison has confirmed to TMZ that the&nbsp;
cause of death had been a fentanyl overdose.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.600
Harrison told TMZ:

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.920
"Yes, I can confirm Adam died from&nbsp;
a fentanyl overdose. The fentanyl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:36.760
crisis in this country must be taken&nbsp;
more seriously. It seems it is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.640
flowing over the borders and nothing is&nbsp;
being done about it. We must do better."

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.240
As TMZ also notes, the Harrison family&nbsp;
previously released a statement asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.520
they be given time amongst themselves to&nbsp;
grapple with this devastating news. It said:

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:57.880
"Our family is extremely saddened by the death of&nbsp;
Adam. We ask for privacy as we grieve his loss."

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.920
Fans of "Pawn Stars" know that while&nbsp;
Rick Harrison's family is fairly active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.560
in his business — including his sons&nbsp;
Corey and Jake — Adam Harrison did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.120
appear on the History Channel series.&nbsp;
Before the show began airing in 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.640
Adam was reportedly involved in the family&nbsp;
business. However, according to his brother Corey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.160
he later branched out and pursued a career as&nbsp;
a plumber, living his life independently and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.560
avoiding the nearly inescapable fame that comes&nbsp;
with appearing on such a hit television series.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:32.440
While fans and friends of the Harrison family are&nbsp;
certainly respecting their wishes for privacy at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:37.000
this time, there was still an outpouring&nbsp;
of support on social media regarding Adam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.240
Harrison's tragic passing. When Rick Harrison&nbsp;
posted an Instagram tribute to his late son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.600
fans responded to the post with well wishes.&nbsp;
Radio host Joe Pags wrote in a comment,

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.600
"Sorry to hear about this, Rick. Sending prayers."

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.280
Actor Emilio Rivera, known for&nbsp;
his roles in "Flamin' Hot" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.960
"Mayans M.C.," paid his respects as well, writing,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.000
"Rest in Peace ... My Condolences&nbsp;
to You and the Familia."

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.520
Donald Trump Jr., son of former president&nbsp;
Donald Trump, also responded to the post,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.160
simply writing, "I'm so sorry man."

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.360
It wasn't just famous figures who&nbsp;
turned up to show their support for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.360
Rick. Fans of the show also offered their&nbsp;
condolences, with one person writing:

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:24.160
"I am so sorry for your loss Rick. Too many of our&nbsp;
children are taken by the disease of addiction."

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.400
Corey Harrison, who was just&nbsp;
one year older than his brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.960
also took to Instagram to post a tribute with&nbsp;
a picture of the pair when they were younger.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:38.120
As Rick Harrison noted in his statement to TMZ,&nbsp;
the ongoing fentanyl and opioid addiction crisis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:44.280
in the United States is nothing short of horrific.&nbsp;
According to the National Institute on Drug Abuse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.640
death rates from overdoses have been&nbsp;
steadily increasing in recent years.

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.160
"We're in a crisis where they don't even&nbsp;
really have an answer. As a result of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.480
more and more people are going to die."

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.200
Because of that, it's not surprising&nbsp;
that pop culture has tried to unpack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.120
this devastating situation, whether&nbsp;
these projects approach the subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.480
head-on or create some sort&nbsp;
of allegorical narrative.

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:12.040
Hulu's "Dopesick" tackles the battle&nbsp;
against Purdue Pharma and their marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:17.800
of OxyContin. Netflix's "Painkiller" also&nbsp;
discusses Purdue but directly adapts its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.560
story from Patrick Radden Keefe's New&nbsp;
Yorker article "The Family That Built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.600
an Empire of Pain" and Barry Meier's book,&nbsp;
"Pain Killer: An Empire of Deceit and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:31.640
Origin of America's Opioid Epidemic,"&nbsp;
which zeroes in on the Sackler family.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.640
Even in the horror genre, Mike Flanagan&nbsp;
addresses the Sacklers in "The Fall of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:40.240
the House of Usher," modeling the fictional&nbsp;
Usher family after the Sacklers and showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.840
just how their corporate greed led to&nbsp;
widespread addiction and tragic death.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.520
It's awful to see yet another promising life cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.000
short in real life when it comes&nbsp;
to Adam Harrison's sudden passing.

